      },
      "reply": "Prescription drug coverage is called Part D. With Original Medicare, drug coverage is separate—you would usually join a standalone drug plan. With Medicare Advantage, most plans include drug coverage as part of the plan.\n\nIn either path, medicine coverage is not automatic for every drug: each plan has its own drug list, pharmacy rules, and costs, so the exact medicines and pharmacies need to be checked before relying on a plan. If you want, next I can explain the practical pros and cons of getting drug coverage separately versus having it built into a Medicare Advantage plan.\n\nWould it help if I compare separate Part D with Original Medicare versus drug coverage built into most Medicare Advantage plans?",
